Subject: [PATCH 0018] Present decoded frames through a Wayland surface

The stream spends almost all of its GPU time turning the decoder's NV12 frame
into RGBA and then sampling that copy into the scene graph. NV12 is exactly the
format the display controller's overlay plane accepts, and the compositor can
import a DMA-BUF without the GPU touching a pixel, which is how a hardware
decoding sink reaches 1080p60 on this board.

record_frame now hands the decoder's DMA-BUF to the client instead of converting
it when OPENNOW_WAYLAND_VIDEO is set: it duplicates the descriptor, reports the
two planes, and returns no GPU image. A new WaylandVideoSurface, built from
Qt's own compositor connection with xdg-shell and linux-dmabuf, creates a
fullscreen window for the stream and attaches one buffer per frame. The window
ignores input so game input keeps travelling the path that already forwards it,
and it is destroyed when the presentation tears down.

This is the foundation for the stream window owning its own presentation; the
controls and the close-to-stop handling follow in the next patch.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [STM32MP25 performance work]

